package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Extended 21-part original mystery serial. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch37 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_bell_beneath_the_library",
            title = "The Bell Beneath the Library",
            tagline = "A brass bell, an old atlas, and a community story waiting to be heard.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Bell Beneath the Library", listOf(
                    "When Imani volunteered to catalogue the town library's donated books, she found a brass bell wrapped in a page from an old atlas. The bell had no clapper, yet a faint mark around its rim suggested it had once been rung often. On the atlas page, someone had drawn a blue square over the river district and written: LISTEN AT LOW TIDE. Imani showed the discovery to her friend Dara, who was more interested in the handwriting than the bell. It matched a signature on a library book returned forty years late.",
                    "The librarian, Mr Sola, recognised the name as that of Ada Nwosu, a local architect who had designed the first public reading rooms. Ada's plans had been lost after a flood, and the town's history display barely mentioned her. Mr Sola allowed the students to study the donated material under supervision. He warned them not to treat a mysterious note as permission to explore closed buildings or restricted areas. Imani agreed. Their search would use public records, interviews, and safe visits arranged with the library."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "A Sound on the Map", listOf(
                    "The atlas page showed a square beside an old ferry landing. Modern maps labelled the place as a public garden, but an archive photograph showed a small reading pavilion there. Dara enlarged the photograph and noticed a bell mounted beside its entrance. A group of children stood beneath it, holding books. The caption said the bell announced the start of the afternoon reading hour. Imani wondered why Ada had hidden a clue in a book donation instead of leaving it with the library records.",
                    "At the archive, they found a newspaper report about a flood that damaged the pavilion. The article said the bell had been recovered, but it did not say where it went. A second report described a community fund created to rebuild the reading room. The fund's final balance was never published. The students wrote down both facts and left the unanswered question open. They were learning that an investigation became stronger when it admitted what it did not yet know."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "The Retired Caretaker", listOf(
                    "Mr Bako, the retired garden caretaker, remembered the pavilion. He said Ada had asked him to keep a small collection of books dry during the flood repairs. The books were moved to several homes, then gradually returned to the library. He could not remember the bell's final location, but he recalled that Ada used it during outdoor reading sessions. He also remembered a wooden cabinet with a blue square painted inside its door.",
                    "Imani asked whether he had any photographs or letters. Mr Bako brought out a folder of garden notices and a picture of the pavilion after the flood. In the background, the cabinet stood beside a temporary shelter. A handwritten label on it read EAST ROOM. Mr Bako explained that the shelter had been used as a reading room while repairs continued. The clue pointed to a place that no longer existed, but its records might still survive."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The East Room Register", listOf(
                    "The library's storage register listed a temporary East Room collection. The students found the entry with help from Mr Sola. It recorded forty-seven books, one cabinet, and a brass bell. Beside the bell was a note: returned to donor after restoration. The donor's name had been covered by a strip of paper that had become brittle with age. The record gave them a date and a reference number, enough to request the linked correspondence.",
                    "The archive clerk found a box of restoration letters. One letter from Ada thanked a group of volunteers for repairing the cabinet. Another asked that the bell be returned to the library when a permanent reading pavilion was built. The pavilion had never been rebuilt, so the bell's return condition had never been met. Imani realised that the bell might have remained with a volunteer family, waiting for a project that was cancelled."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Family Photograph", listOf(
                    "The volunteer list included the surname Okorie. Dara knew a retired music teacher with that name, Mrs Okorie, who lived near the market. They arranged a visit with her permission and showed her the photograph. She recognised her grandfather beside the cabinet. Her family had kept a brass object for years, but she did not know it was the library bell. She offered to look for the old family inventory before making any decision about it.",
                    "The next afternoon, Mrs Okorie called the library. The inventory described a 'reading bell, held in trust until a permanent room is ready'. The wording matched the archive letter. She said her family would be happy to return it if the library could document its history and acknowledge the volunteers who had cared for it. Imani was relieved. The mystery was becoming a story of trust rather than a hunt for something to take."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Room That Never Opened", listOf(
                    "The students searched the council's public planning records for the permanent pavilion. They found two proposals, both approved in principle, but neither had received final funding. A note explained that the garden site had been reassigned for flood drainage improvements. The reading room was not rejected because people disliked it; the project had been postponed while the district dealt with a serious practical need.",
                    "Dara drew a timeline showing the flood, temporary room, proposed pavilion, and later drainage work. The bell's long absence now made sense. It had been kept safely because the promise to return it was tied to a building that was never constructed. The students decided their final project should not imply that the drainage work was a mistake. They wanted to honour the reading room while recognising why the land had changed."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Hidden Ledger", listOf(
                    "A note in the restoration letters mentioned a ledger kept by the volunteers. Mr Sola checked the library's donation catalogue and found a ledger listed under community records. Its pages recorded book purchases, transport costs, and small donations. Near the end, the volunteers had set aside money for shelves in the future pavilion. The amount was modest, but every contribution had a name beside it.",
                    "One page listed a final transfer to the library's general rebuilding fund. The money had not disappeared; it had helped replace books damaged by the flood. The students verified the transfer against a receipt stored in the same folder. They were glad to have an answer, but they also understood that the ledger represented many people's effort. They asked the librarian whether the names could be included in the exhibition with permission."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The Bell's Missing Voice", listOf(
                    "Mrs Okorie brought the bell to the library in a padded box. It was carefully examined by the collections volunteer, who confirmed that it matched the old photograph. Its clapper was missing, which explained why it made no sound when moved. A conservator said the bell should not be repaired until its metal and age had been assessed. The students agreed that preserving it mattered more than making it ring for their presentation.",
                    "Imani noticed a tiny inscription around the base: READ TOGETHER. It had been difficult to see beneath years of dust. Mr Sola explained that the phrase was used by the original reading volunteers. The bell had not been a signal of authority; it had been a welcoming sound that gathered children for stories. The students chose to make the inscription the centre of their display."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "A Different Kind of Opening", listOf(
                    "The library director proposed a small display rather than a new building project. The display would include the bell, atlas page, photograph, volunteer ledger, and a map showing where the pavilion had stood. Imani suggested adding audio recordings of former readers describing what the outdoor sessions meant to them. The director liked the idea but asked the students to obtain consent and offer written alternatives for visitors who preferred not to be recorded.",
                    "Mrs Okorie contacted two former volunteers. One remembered learning to read from picture books beneath the pavilion roof. Another remembered carrying boxes to the temporary East Room after the flood. Their accounts differed in detail but shared a sense of belonging. Imani typed each story exactly as approved and included the speakers' preferred names. The project was now preserving voices as carefully as it preserved objects."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Archive's Correction", listOf(
                    "While preparing the display labels, the students noticed that the town history booklet credited the pavilion design to an unnamed council committee. Ada's signed plan was in the archive, but the booklet did not mention her. Mr Sola submitted a correction request with copies of the plan and restoration letters. The archive board reviewed the evidence and agreed to update the online catalogue before the exhibition opened.",
                    "The correction did not erase the work of the builders or volunteers. The new entry named Ada as the architect and listed the community members whose records documented the reading sessions. Imani was pleased that the record had become more accurate without turning the project into a contest over credit. Dara added a note explaining how the correction had been verified, so future researchers could follow the evidence."
                )),
                StoryPart(11, "A Stormy Rehearsal", listOf(
                    "The week before the exhibition, heavy rain closed the garden path and forced the library to move the display rehearsal indoors. The students worried that the map would lose its impact without the actual site visible through the windows. Mr Sola reminded them that the story was about a community resource, not a particular view. They rearranged the panels so visitors could understand the place through photographs, maps, and voices.",
                    "The rehearsal revealed another issue: the audio recordings were too quiet for the busy room. The students added headphones and printed transcripts. They also made a large-print version of the labels. Mrs Okorie tested the display and suggested placing the bell at a height where wheelchair users could see its inscription. The students adjusted the layout. Each change made the exhibition more welcoming."
                )),
                StoryPart(12, "The Blue Square", listOf(
                    "Dara compared the atlas page with the original plan and noticed that the blue square was not placed over the pavilion itself. It marked a nearby water channel that had carried floodwater away from the homes. The note 'listen at low tide' referred to the time when the channel could be inspected safely from the public walkway, not to a hidden object. The clue had guided Ada's old volunteers to a place where they could check the drainage after storms.",
                    "The students asked the council's drainage officer to explain the channel. He confirmed that the route had been maintained and that the garden's redesign helped protect nearby houses. He thanked them for checking before making claims. Imani added a panel showing how the public garden, reading pavilion, and drainage system had shared the same small area over time. The history was more connected than anyone had expected."
                )),
                StoryPart(13, "The Message in the Margin", listOf(
                    "The handwriting on the atlas page was Ada's, but the phrase had been added years after the original map was printed. A dated note in the archive showed that Ada had written it for the volunteers, asking them to check the channel after a high tide and report any blockage. The bell had been wrapped in the page when the family packed the temporary room's belongings. It was an ordinary instruction that had survived because someone kept the paper.",
                    "Imani felt a little disappointed that there was no secret chamber or hidden treasure. Then Mrs Okorie told her that the real surprise was how many people had quietly protected the library's history. The students decided to include the note's practical meaning in the exhibition. A mystery did not need a dramatic secret to matter. Sometimes it revealed the care that had been present all along."
                )),
                StoryPart(14, "The Community Review", listOf(
                    "Before opening day, the students invited former volunteers, current library users, and the drainage team to review the display. One visitor corrected a date on a photograph. Another identified a child in the picture but asked that the name not be published. The students changed the caption and kept the person's identity private. The drainage officer checked the map and confirmed that the channel was shown in the right place.",
                    "The review improved the exhibition. It also showed the students that public history was a shared responsibility. They made a clear distinction between documents, memories, and interpretations. Where accounts differed, the display said so rather than choosing the most exciting version. Mr Sola praised their careful labels. The bell rested in its case, silent but surrounded by the voices of people who remembered what it had meant."
                )),
                StoryPart(15, "Opening Day", listOf(
                    "Visitors arrived early on opening day. Mrs Okorie stood beside the display while Mr Sola welcomed the first group. Imani explained how the atlas page led to the East Room register, how the register led to the family inventory, and how the ledger revealed what happened to the rebuilding fund. Dara showed the timeline and pointed out which details came from documents and which came from interviews.",
                    "A young visitor asked whether the bell could be rung. The conservator explained that it was safer to preserve it as it was. Imani offered to play a recording of a similar bell used in a public reading session, clearly labelled as a reconstruction. The visitor listened, then read the inscription. 'Read together,' she said. Imani realised that the message still worked even when the bell remained quiet."
                )),
                StoryPart(16, "The Book Table Returns", listOf(
                    "The library director announced a new monthly outdoor reading table in the garden, placed well away from the drainage channel and approved by the council. It would not pretend to rebuild the old pavilion. Instead, it would continue the part of its purpose that people missed most: sharing books in a welcoming public place. Volunteers signed up to organise the first session, and the library offered books for different ages and reading levels.",
                    "Mrs Okorie donated a sturdy box for transporting the books. Mr Sola created a rota so the work would not fall on one person. Imani and Dara designed a simple sign using the blue square from the atlas page. They included a note explaining that the symbol came from Ada's map and represented the community's care for both learning and the neighbourhood."
                )),
                StoryPart(17, "A Question from the Past", listOf(
                    "A week after the exhibition opened, the archive received an envelope from a former volunteer who had moved away. Inside was a letter written by Ada, thanking the group for keeping the temporary reading room active. The letter mentioned that the bell should return to the library when a permanent room was ready, but it also said the most important part of the project was the habit of reading together. The letter confirmed the meaning the students had inferred from the records.",
                    "The archive added the letter to the collection after checking its provenance with the sender. Imani updated the display's final panel and credited the person who had preserved the document. The new evidence did not change the central story, but it gave the volunteers' work a clearer voice. Mr Sola said the exhibition could remain open as a small permanent collection because it had become useful to the public."
                )),
                StoryPart(18, "The First Reading Hour", listOf(
                    "The new garden reading table opened on a bright Saturday. Families arrived with mats, folding chairs, and books they wanted to share. The library volunteers arranged the collection by age and interest. No one rang the historic bell; it remained safely in the library. Instead, a student volunteer read the first line from a new story and invited everyone to join. The simple beginning felt right.",
                    "Imani watched a child choose a book and sit beside an older neighbour. She thought about the people in the old photograph and how the same small act had continued across generations. The reading table was not the pavilion, and the garden was not unchanged, but the purpose had found a new shape. The students took photographs for the archive and asked permission before including anyone in the record."
                )),
                StoryPart(19, "A Map for Tomorrow", listOf(
                    "The class created a new map showing the library, garden reading table, drainage channel, archive, and the former pavilion site. It included accessible routes and clear notes about areas that were not public. The map was added to the library website as a downloadable guide. Mr Adeyemi, a volunteer cartographer, reviewed the labels and praised the students for showing both old and new features without confusing them.",
                    "Imani placed a copy beside the historic atlas page. The old map recorded what had been; the new one showed how the neighbourhood had changed and what people were building now. The blue square appeared on both, but its meaning had grown. It no longer pointed only to a clue. It marked a promise to keep learning, caring, and remembering."
                )),
                StoryPart(20, "The Bell's New Home", listOf(
                    "The library board approved a permanent case for the bell and a rotating space for community stories. Mrs Okorie attended the small handover ceremony with her family. The director thanked them for preserving the object and explained that it would remain available for study. A digital record linked the bell to Ada's plans, the volunteer ledger, and the memories collected during the project.",
                    "Imani gave the board a copy of the students' research notes. She had included every source, the dates checked, the permissions received, and the questions that remained unanswered. The board asked to use the notes as an example for future student researchers. Dara joked that their work had become part of the library's instructions. Imani thought that was a good ending for a story about a library."
                )),
                StoryPart(21, "Twenty-One Small Lights", listOf(
                    "Months later, the library held a celebration for the volunteers who had supported the reading table. The historic bell stood behind glass while a new handbell announced the start of the session. Imani and Dara were invited to speak. They thanked the people who had trusted them with photographs, letters, and memories, and reminded visitors that the flood, the drainage project, and the reading room all belonged to the same history.",
                    "After the event, Imani returned the atlas page to its protective folder. She no longer wondered whether it concealed a secret. It had carried a practical instruction, a memory, and a path between people who cared about their neighbourhood. The bell had not needed to ring to be heard. Its story had gathered the community again, and the reading hour would continue long after the students moved on."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
